    "mfr_lb_geek_cards_yaWMLy": {
      "type": "mfr-lb__geek-cards",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_j6zhyR": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The 4 Pillars of [highlight]Probiotics[/highlight]",
            "disable_animation": false,
            "header_style": "h1",
            "make_xl": "",
            "highlight_color": "#f7c127",
            "underline_color": "",
            "max_width": 30
          }
        },
        "text_F7pBy4": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>2500 years ago, Hippocrates said “All Disease Begins in The Gut”, and therefore so does healing it.</p>",
            "disable_animation": false,
            "text_style": "xlarge",
            "color": "",
            "max_width": 37
          }
        },
        "entry_VMkG7w": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Immunity",
            "caption": "About 70% of our  immune system resides in the gut. Probiotics like Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG and Bifidobacterium lactis have been shown to enhance immune response by modulating gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) and promoting the production of immune cells such as T-cells (Frontiers in Immunology, 2020). [geek-text]<br>An imbalanced gut with not enough of the good bacteria means you are likely to get sick more often and for longer periods of time.[/geek-text]",
            "bg_color": "#ffb9a6",
            "text_color": "#2b3990"
          }
        },
        "entry_NymQHy": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Skin",
            "caption": "Probiotics enhance skin health by modulating the gut-skin axis, which influences skin conditions such as acne, eczema, and rosacea.[geek-text]<br>Studies indicate that gut microbiota dysbiosis can exacerbate inflammatory skin disorders, while probiotic supplementation helps restore balance, reducing inflammation and promoting skin barrier function (Bowe et al., 2014; Sivan et al., 2020). Probiotics improve hydration, strengthen the skin barrier, and protect against environmental damage by regulating gut health, thus fostering clearer and more resilient skin (Zhang et al., 2020)[geek-text]",
            "bg_color": "#4fd4da",
            "text_color": "#2b3990"
          }
        },
        "entry_YEe3qz": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Brain Health",
            "caption": "A balanced gut microbiome has a profound impact on brain health and mood. 95% of serotonin (the brain’s feel good chemical) is made in the gut. If you suffer from an imbalanced microbiome with not enough good bacteria it will have a profound impact on the brain and mood.[geek-text]<br>The gut-brain axis connects the gut and brain, influencing mental well-being. Probiotics can help reduce symptoms of anxiety, stress, and depression by supporting gut health and the production of neurotransmitters like serotonin. Emerging research on the gut-brain axis shows that probiotics can influence mood and reduce symptoms of anxiety and depression by enhancing gut health and serotonin production. Studies show that probiotics such as Lactobacillus helveticus and Bifidobacterium longum can support mental well-being by improving gut microbiome balance (Psychiatry Research, 2017).[/geek-text]",
            "bg_color": "#ffcb36",
            "text_color": "#2b3990"
          }
        },
        "entry_BNt9Ww": {
          "type": "entry",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Gut Health",
            "caption": "Probiotics help maintain a healthy balance of good bacteria in the gut, which is essential for digestion, nutrient absorption, and gut integrity. A balanced gut microbiome can prevent digestive issues like bloating, constipation, and gas.[geek-text]<br>Probiotics help regulate digestion and maintain gut motility, reducing the risk of gastrointestinal conditions like irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), diarrhea, and acid reflux.Probiotics help maintain a healthy balance of gut bacteria, crucial for proper digestion and nutrient absorption. Research shows that a balanced gut microbiome is linked to improved digestion, reduced inflammation, and better gut barrier function (Journal of Clinical Gastroenterology, 2019).Probiotics have been shown to help reduce the risk of chronic diseases, including cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes, by reducing inflammation, improving cholesterol levels, and supporting metabolic health (Nature Reviews Endocrinology, 2020).[/geek-text]",
            "bg_color": "#ffe9e2",
            "text_color": "#2b3990"
          }
        },
